    "lawCode": "Amd §2, add Art 11 §§170 - 173, Indian L",
    "lawSection": "Indian Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S6889\n\nSPONSOR: PALUMBO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the Indian law, in relation to the state recognition and\nacknowledgement of the Montaukett Indians\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nProvides for the State recognition and acknowledgement of the Montaukett\nIndians.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Amends section 2 of the Indian law to include the Montaukett\nIndians within the term \"Indian nation or tribe\" in New York\n\nSection 2. Amends the Indian law by adding a new article 11 relating to\nthe State recognition and acknowledgement of the Montaukett Indians.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Native American Montaukett Tribe has a long, culturally rich history\non the East End of Long Island. The Montaukett Indians had been formally\nrecognized by the State of New York, until their acknowledgement was\nquestionably removed in 1910 in the land claim case of Pharoah v.Benson\n69 Misc. Rep. 241 (Supreme, Suffolk co., 1910) affirmed 164 App. Div.\n51, affirmed 222 N.Y. 665, when the Montaukett Indians were declared to\nbe extinct. This case was subsequently described in a 1994 State Supreme\nCourt case, Breakers Motel, Inc. v. Sunbeach Montauk Two, Inc. as being\nof 'questionable proriety'.\n\nHistorically, the New York State Legislature has granted recognition or\nacknowledgment to Indian nations and tribes in the State of New York by\nan act of the Legislature. Just as Chapter 31 of the Laws of 10-0 and\nChapter 39 of the Laws of 1978 established Article 9 and Article 10 of\nthe Indian Law that include two other eastern Long Island Nations, the\nShinnecock and Poospatuck (Unkechauge), respectively.\n\nIn 2013, the Governor vetoed a measure passed by the legislature that\nwould have established criteria for considering the request of the\nMontauketts to be acknowledged by the State. Veto  210 of 2013 included\na directive by the Governor to the Department of State (DOS) to move\nforward with an analysis to determine if State recognition of the\nMontaukett Indians was warranted.\n\nAfter four years had gone by still no determination by DOS in this\nregard, the Legislature passed legislation in 2017 to provide State\nrecognition and acknowledgment of the Montaukett Indians. The Governor\nwent on to issue another veto stating that analysis was still ongoing\nand directed the Department to \"continue to proactively communicate with\nthe Montaukett Indian Tribe, as well as any interested parties to obtain\nall relevant information necessary to complete the review in a timely\nmanner\". Again, in 2018, the Governor vetoed this initiative as the DOS\nwas still receiving supporting documentation from the interested groups\nand wanted to \"allow the administrative process to take its due course\".\n\n\nIt has now been well over a century since the courts of New York State\ndeclared that the Montaukett Indians no longer existed. This misdeed has\nbeen perpetuated over all these years despite the fact that the members\nof this sovereign Indian nation have continued to live in our community,\nmaintain their culture and govern themselves. It is time for the DOS to\nconclude their analysis and favorable recommend that this long overdue\nlegislation become a state law.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019-20 S.3691/A.5411; 2018 S.7770/A.9898 (Veto #360); 2017\nS.3006/A.2325 (Veto #210); 2015-16 S.375A/A.202A; 2014 S.7619/A.9704\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
